WEBVTT

00:00:23.356 --> 00:00:25.596
What kind of animals hatch from eggs?

00:00:25.852 --> 00:00:27.852
but can gallop like a horse.

00:00:27.932 --> 00:00:29.532
or swim across an ocean.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:38.020
The same animals that can go a year between meals, and live for a century,

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:43.770
and who have patiently watched as the dinosaurs have come and gone.

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:47.940
Few diseases, it seems, can touch them.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:51.640
And with hearts the most advanced of any animal on Earth,

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:55.110
they hold the key to critical scientific advance.

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:59.320
High-performance predators.

00:01:00.316 --> 00:01:01.556
They remain still.

00:01:02.076 --> 00:01:04.076
masters of their domain.

00:01:52.236 --> 00:01:53.216
in northern Australia.

00:01:55.148 --> 00:01:59.148
Trees and bushes along its banks are home to a noisy colony of fruit bats.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:06.820
It's the wet season, and the water is high.

00:02:08.956 --> 00:02:12.296
But the bats are too busy hanging out to notice any danger below.

00:02:21.372 --> 00:02:23.272
Enter nature's supreme opportunist.

00:02:23.868 --> 00:02:25.368
the Saltwater Crocodile.

00:02:27.132 --> 00:02:28.432
A quarter mile downwind.

00:02:28.828 --> 00:02:29.988
The smell and the clamor

00:02:30.156 --> 00:02:31.716
have attracted its attention.

00:02:32.012 --> 00:02:32.252
Thank you.

00:02:39.852 --> 00:02:41.352
Guidance systems lock on.

00:03:07.868 --> 00:03:09.868
cover of water it can approach unseen.

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:18.660
like a U-boat in enemy waters.

00:03:19.068 --> 00:03:21.068
It surfaces without a ripple.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.860
And while the bats chatter, the croc can pick off its target.

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:44.630
Part submarine, part jump jet, a powerful tail of solid muscle can launch it six feet in the air.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:55.030
Mission accomplished.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:10.150
These are the world's largest reptilian predators, sometimes weighing in at over a ton.

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:19.180
Their state-of-the-art design has needed few modifications in nearly a quarter of a billion years.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:29.980
In the water, a vanishing act leaves only a highly sensitive parent.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.290
telescope above the surface. Eyes that see by night as well as day can watch your every

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:47.850
move. Hidden ears are listening in, while barely exposed nostrils complete the surveillance

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:55.410
above deck. But this is the tip of the iceberg. Beneath the surface, this motionless predator

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:59.560
is alert to movement, sensing vibration in the water through its skin.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:11.300
When it's time to dive, the hatches close.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:18.440
A flap at the back of the mouth seals the throat and air passages.

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:24.520
Ears and nostrils go into shutdown, and a see-through membrane wipes over the eyes.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:29.700
Stealth hunters from the water.

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:34.440
They have infiltrated the swamps and rivers of every warm land mass on the planet.

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:42.380
Collectively, they are known as crocodilians, and today there are over 20 distinct species.

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:52.260
Alligators abound in the U.S., while their close cousins, caimans, reach south as far as Argentina.

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:59.730
Cosmopolitan crocodiles are more widely spread, from the Americas to Africa, from tropical

00:05:59.730 --> 00:05:59.970
late.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:06.170
into the Pacific. Indeed, the only gator outside America is a long-lost cousin in

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:16.980
China. The crocodilians are descended from a lineage that branched away from

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:26.050
other reptiles early in evolutionary history. It was a lineage that spawned

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:29.980
not only the crocodilians, but which also gave rise both to dinosaurs and

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.130
and to some more surprising cousins, birds.

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:43.330
So despite a superficial resemblance to lizards,

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:47.020
a crocodile is more closely related to a goose.

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:56.290
There's nothing bird-like about a croc's feeding gear

00:06:56.290 --> 00:06:59.800
in a life that can span a hundred years.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.460
it will wear through several thousand teeth.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:07.320
With 60 plus to a mouth, a croc can nail just about anything.

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:55.750
Jaws are tools for biting with a force of several times.

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:10.540
Yet with very little opening leverage, they can be held shut with a sturdy rubber band.

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:15.840
The alligator is the consummate crushing machine,

00:08:16.390 --> 00:08:20.690
its broad, rounded snout ideal for cracking turtles or crabs.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.880
For most crocodiles, though, narrower, pointed jaws are better suited to a highly varied

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:39.010
diet, from tiny prawns to hefty wildebeest.

00:08:41.660 --> 00:08:44.040
The difference, though, is more than a matter of shape.

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:49.380
Unlike alligators, crocodiles are adapted to survive at sea.

00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:55.130
Glands in the tongue shed excess salt, keeping body fluids in balance.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:59.860
crocodiles can cruise around coastlines in search of new rivers.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:01.940
but a few go further.

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:12.320
Saltwater crocodiles are truly Olympic swimmers,

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:15.720
sometimes making epic journeys of hundreds of miles

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:17.680
to reach remote oceanic islands.

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:25.950
Neither croc nor alligator,

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:29.700
a strange creature basks on the banks of an Indian river.

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:41.450
A gharial. With its ugly, bulbous nose, the male can grow to nearly 20 feet, but he is harmless to humans.

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:51.000
Rapier jaws set with needle-sharp teeth make lethal weapons for snatching slippery fish, and a gharial eats little else.

00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:59.160
Ninety days under warm sand, and a gharial's eggs have hatched into a noisy family.

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:04.930
Curiously, the sex of crocodiles depends not on their genes, but on temperature.

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:08.480
Keep it moderate during incubation, and you'll breed daughters.

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:16.580
For males, let's say, some like it hot, but not too hot.

00:10:18.810 --> 00:10:23.150
In nests that overheat, the hatchlings are baked into grotesque shapes.

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:50.260
The past 20-odd million years have brought an ever-changing menu to the resident crocodiles,

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:56.010
not always in manageable portions or easy to pluck from the bank.

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:05.640
Even in the water, it can be difficult to make a meal of it.

00:11:08.660 --> 00:11:11.600
These are Nile crocodiles, and despite their name,

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:15.320
they can be found throughout the river systems and swamps of tropical Africa.

00:11:21.810 --> 00:11:25.050
It is late October, and the dry season is wearing on.

00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:34.840
Thirsty animals have no choice but to take their chances at the water's edge.

00:11:44.840 --> 00:11:48.560
Sticky mud can turn a quick drink into a hair-raising gauntlet.

00:12:14.140 --> 00:12:24.870
point-blank range, it can strike at 20 miles an hour, repeated by a hair's breadth.

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:32.580
But this is a river swimming with opportunities.

00:13:00.030 --> 00:13:03.130
Wise to the danger, their mother has sounded the alarm.

00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:08.720
An old hippo lies dying in shallow water.

00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:13.780
He may be starving to death, but he's not the only one who's hungry.

00:13:39.880 --> 00:13:45.480
But even a dying hippo has a lethal bite, and for the famished lion, it's better to wait.

00:13:54.590 --> 00:13:58.510
Twilight descends, the heat lifts, and cool breezes begin to stir.

00:14:07.450 --> 00:14:13.030
While most reptiles are active only by day, crocodiles don't stop when the sun goes down.

00:14:33.650 --> 00:14:40.980
The night breeze wafts a stench of rotting flesh, and the promise of a feast.

00:14:43.010 --> 00:14:52.900
It's the end for the old hippo, and hungry animals gather on the banks, but the water

00:14:52.900 --> 00:14:54.360
is strictly off limits.

00:14:57.490 --> 00:14:59.930
This is one for the crocs, and...

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:01.680
they won't be pussyfooting around.

00:15:32.780 --> 00:15:37.750
Perhaps it looks greedy, but a crocodile eats in surprisingly dainty portions.

00:15:38.930 --> 00:15:41.670
Its stomach is not much bigger than a human head.

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:51.780
Morning, and the crocodiles have one thing on their mind.

00:15:53.560 --> 00:15:58.140
Their bellies may be full, but to digest their meal, they must first turn up the oven.

00:16:01.400 --> 00:16:04.280
Like all reptiles, they're only up to speed when they're warm.

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:07.900
Food will literally rot inside a cold crock.

00:16:10.350 --> 00:16:13.480
So for once, sunbathing really is the healthy option.

00:16:18.380 --> 00:16:23.460
While a crock relaxes, hidden structures within its back are busy gathering heat.

00:16:25.640 --> 00:16:29.150
This is a basking alligator, as the human eye sees it.

00:16:31.280 --> 00:16:33.980
But a heat-sensitive camera reveals something more.

00:16:34.830 --> 00:16:37.630
Built-in solar panels deployed along its back.

00:16:41.180 --> 00:16:43.820
Raised bumps glow warmer than the rest of the body,

00:16:48.140 --> 00:16:50.880
for these miniature peaks are trapping the sun's warmth.

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:57.060
Each incorporates a bony disc laced with tiny blood vessels.

00:16:57.940 --> 00:16:59.480
Heat is absorbed through the skin.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:01.700
and downloaded into the bloodstream.

00:17:08.030 --> 00:17:10.130
Maximum comfort for most crocodilians

00:17:10.130 --> 00:17:12.630
is somewhere around 89 degrees Fahrenheit,

00:17:16.590 --> 00:17:19.740
much hotter, and they simply move to shade or water.

00:17:25.940 --> 00:17:28.720
By shifting between cool water and a warm riverbank,

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:29.900
they can control...

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:39.770
body temperature to within a few degrees. It's old-fashioned to call them cold-blooded.

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:46.960
Crocodilians, we now know, are adept at keeping warm, and their efficient use of solar power

00:17:47.110 --> 00:17:55.330
hands them a winning advantage over other animals. Rain or shine, birds and mammals

00:17:55.330 --> 00:17:58.890
warm their bodies from the inside by burning fuel.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:04.740
It's a passport to cold climates, yet they must work busily to keep the furnaces stoked.

00:18:07.280 --> 00:18:15.770
Pound for pound, a bird uses over 20 times more energy than a crock, guzzles up to 50 times more food.

00:18:16.790 --> 00:18:22.060
To a basking gator, a bird's life must seem one long feeding frenzy.

00:18:22.200 --> 00:18:28.640
By drawing free heat from the sun, the gator's food needs are slashed.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:36.680
Crocs can even cruise on their fat stores for a year. These guys were eco-friendly long before

00:18:36.680 --> 00:18:47.960
it was fashionable. Little wonder then that they seem so laid back. They're playing a waiting game

00:18:47.960 --> 00:18:56.640
that would kill the rest of us. However, don't be taken in by the casual air. Crocs can erupt with

00:18:56.640 --> 00:18:57.460
awesome power.

00:19:23.290 --> 00:19:29.980
they can also plod for miles, seeking water if their home creek dries up. Here they move

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:36.730
more with the high four-legged walk of a mammal than the sprawl or slither of a reptile. Yet

00:19:36.730 --> 00:19:43.020
this advanced formula movement is neither mammal nor reptile. It's unique to the croc,

00:19:43.600 --> 00:19:53.440
and it's a tail in two parts. Croc's armored skin makes a rugged flak jacket,

00:19:53.940 --> 00:19:59.980
but it also plays a surprising structural role. When we started to dissect...

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:06.380
this animal, we found that removing this segment of skin was really difficult because all the

00:20:06.380 --> 00:20:12.540
muscles running down the back are attached directly to the underside of the armor. And

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:19.160
you can see that better from underneath on this segment. Each segment of muscle runs

00:20:19.160 --> 00:20:24.740
in a different direction. And what this means is that when they contract, they brace the

00:20:24.740 --> 00:20:26.510
armor against the backbone.

00:20:36.430 --> 00:20:39.890
But his versatile armor is more than just an anchor plate for muscles.

00:20:40.540 --> 00:20:41.940
It's the key to movement.

00:20:59.430 --> 00:20:59.750
Gravity.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:06.880
pulls us all down. To keep from sagging, an animal like a cow has muscles that spring

00:21:06.880 --> 00:21:15.280
the backbone. In crocodiles, though, the muscles operate differently.

00:21:17.010 --> 00:21:23.240
If muscles bulge, they expand. But in the case of crocodilians, they are contained

00:21:23.240 --> 00:21:29.290
in a sort of tube, which is formed by armor and the backbone.

00:21:30.090 --> 00:21:35.010
and this system can be compared to a hose running alongside the body which is filled with water

00:21:35.010 --> 00:21:41.790
and like a hose which is turned on the whole body is stiffened by the inner pressure of the bulging

00:21:41.790 --> 00:21:59.970
Muscles so crocodiles use a unique lifting system that exploits

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:04.820
hydraulic pressure, and with body raised, they can do better than just walk.

00:22:40.840 --> 00:22:45.500
Wild tales aside, no crocodile living today will run you down for dinner.

00:22:54.590 --> 00:22:59.960
Yet this is just how crocodiles began their evolutionary career over 200 million years ago.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:06.880
years ago, small prototype species, no more than a few feet long, would indeed have run

00:23:06.880 --> 00:23:18.390
down their prey. Later designs even incorporated stubby, hoof-like toes. These bounding crocs

00:23:18.390 --> 00:23:28.210
must have been predators from hell. Yet it was the long jaws and low profile of the

00:23:28.210 --> 00:23:29.970
sit-and-wait hunter lurking at the...

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:35.810
water's edge that proved the winning design. It's a formula that has been used by many

00:23:35.810 --> 00:23:44.760
different forms, like this colossal Purosaurus, 35-foot caiman with a bucket-sized nostril

00:23:44.760 --> 00:23:51.900
on its snout. But imagine the ultimate, a crocodile the size of Tyrannosaurus rex. This

00:23:51.900 --> 00:23:57.920
was a Terminator with box office dimensions, a dinosaur killer, from the tidal swamps

00:23:57.920 --> 00:23:59.920
of Mississippi, Dinosuchus.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:16.880
was seven tons of pure predator, another 70 million years of evolution.

00:24:17.620 --> 00:24:22.500
And while the cast of characters has changed, the plot has remained exactly the same.

00:24:22.780 --> 00:24:26.700
Croc biologist Dr. Graham Webb has the scars to prove it.

